PROPOSAL — not shipped, not applied by `weld add cron`.

Target: capabilities/base/files/app.go.tmpl (generated internal/app/app.go)
Owner: the base capability.

Why it is not applied here: app.go is the base scaffold, shared by every
capability, and cron must not edit it.

The base app dispatches a command and returns: there is no shared runtime that
long-running commands start, so today the scheduler has to be wired into each
serve command individually (see http_serve.snippet and loom_graph.snippet). If
more long-running capabilities appear, a small runtime seam keeps "follow the
long-running command, not help/version" in one place:

    // Runtime is work that follows a long-running command's lifecycle. Short
    // commands (help, version, an unknown command) never construct or start one.
    type Runtime interface {
        Start(ctx context.Context) error
        Stop(ctx context.Context) error
    }

    var runtimes []Runtime

    // RegisterRuntime appends a runtime in registration order.
    func RegisterRuntime(runtime Runtime) {
        if runtime == nil {
            panic("app: runtime must not be nil")
        }
        runtimes = append(runtimes, runtime)
    }

and a helper a long-running command owns:

    func StartRuntimes(ctx context.Context) (stop func(context.Context) error, err error)

A `serve` command would call StartRuntimes in place of constructing the scheduler
itself; help and version never call it. This is optional and only worth doing if
several capabilities need the same lifecycle, so it is left to the base owner.

Until then, the recommended path is the Loom lifecycle hooks (loom_graph.snippet)
for the Loom composition and the explicit Start/Stop in http_serve.snippet for
the plain one. Both keep the scheduler tied to serve and out of short commands.
